Title: The Tell-Tale Heart
Credit: Written by
Author: Edgar Allan Poe
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/1/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal live-action gothic period drama, naturalistic faces, candlelit interiors, and oppressive nineteenth-century realism.

FADE IN:

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

A narrow, shadowed hall. A closed bedroom door faces the camera; its brass latch glints faintly.

(SOUND: wind murmurs in the chimney and a distant clock ticks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
True!—nervous—very, very dreadfully nervous I had been and am; but why will you say that I am mad?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The disease had sharpened my senses—not destroyed—not dulled them.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Above all was the sense of hearing acute. Hearken! and observe how healthily—how calmly I can tell you the whole story.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

An OLD MAN, thin and stooped, lies beneath a heavy quilt. Wisps of white hair frame his hollow face; one pale blue eye carries a milky film.

(SOUND: old clock mechanism clicks behind the wall)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved the old man. He had never wronged me. He had never given me insult.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For his gold I had no desire. I think it was his eye! yes, it was this!

A dark lantern rests outside the door. Its shutter is tightly closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had the eye of a vulture—a pale blue eye, with a film over it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Whenever it fell upon me, my blood ran cold; and so by degrees I made up my mind to take the life of the old man.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

The bedroom door opens a fraction. The NARRATOR'S hand, pale and steady, turns the latch with exquisite care.

(SOUND: latch barely scrapes; floorboards settle)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Every night, about midnight, I turned the latch of his door and opened it—oh, so gently!

The hand eases the dark lantern through the crack, still sealed against all light.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I put in a dark lantern, all closed, closed, that no light shone out.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I moved it slowly—very, very slowly, so that I might not disturb the old man's sleep.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The OLD MAN sleeps, his filmed eye shut. The lantern's shutter opens only enough for a thread of light that stops short of the bed.

(SOUND: the old man breathes softly; a timber creaks)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For seven long nights—every night just at midnight—but I found the eye always closed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was impossible to do the work; for it was not the old man who vexed me, but his Evil Eye.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - MORNING

Morning light leaks through the shutters. The OLD MAN, still thin and white-haired in his nightshirt, sits upright in bed.

(SOUND: sparrows chirp faintly beyond the shuttered window)

The NARRATOR enters openly, carrying a small breakfast tray. The old man watches him with his pale blue eye.

NARRATOR
How have you passed the night?

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I was never kinder to the old man than during the whole week before I killed him.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He would have been a very profound old man, indeed, to suspect that every night, just at twelve, I looked in upon him while he slept.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - NIGHT

On the eighth night, the NARRATOR'S hand advances the latch more slowly than a watch hand. The dark lantern hangs beneath the wrist.

(SOUND: rain taps the roof; the latch gives a tiny metallic click)

A muffled chuckle escapes from the darkness.

(SOUND: bedsprings creak sharply inside the chamber)

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

The OLD MAN bolts upright in bed. His white hair is disordered; his pale blue eye searches the black room.

(SOUND: the old man's breath catches; the clock ticks loudly)

OLD MAN
Who's there?

The doorway remains black. The NARRATOR does not answer.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
For a whole hour I did not move a muscle.

The OLD MAN sits rigidly, listening toward the door.

(SOUND: a faint groan rises from the bed)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the groan of mortal terror.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I knew what the old man felt, and pitied him, although I chuckled at heart.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
He had been trying to fancy them causeless: “It is nothing but the wind in the chimney—it is only a mouse crossing the floor.”

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - NIGHT

A single dim ray slips from the lantern and lands directly on the OLD MAN'S open eye. The blue iris shines beneath its hideous veil.

(SOUND: a low, muffled heartbeat begins beneath the silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was open—wide, wide open—and I grew furious as I gazed upon it.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I could see nothing else of the old man's face or person. I had directed the ray precisely upon the damned spot.

The NARRATOR'S hand holds the lantern utterly still.

(SOUND: heartbeat quickens, like a watch enveloped in cotton)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There came to my ears a low, dull, quick sound, such as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was the beating of the old man's heart.

The old man trembles under the quilt. The heartbeat grows louder.

(SOUND: heartbeat pounds faster; rain lashes the shutters)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It grew quicker and quicker, and louder and louder every instant.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I thought the heart must burst. A new anxiety seized me—the sound would be heard by a neighbour!

The lantern bursts fully open. The NARRATOR lunges into the room.

(SOUND: the old man gives one piercing shriek)

The old man falls beneath the heavy bed as the NARRATOR heaves it over him.

(SOUND: bedframe crashes onto the floor; the muffled heartbeat continues)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In an instant I dragged him to the floor, and pulled the heavy bed over him.

The NARRATOR kneels beside the bed, waiting. The heartbeat slows, then stops.

(SOUND: the heartbeat fades into complete silence)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
At length it ceased. The old man was dead.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The bed stands aside. Three floor planks lean against the wall. A wooden tub sits beneath the work area.

(SOUND: saw teeth rasp softly through wood; a distant bell begins to toll)

The NARRATOR lifts the planks and lowers wrapped remains into the dark space between the scantlings.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
First of all I dismembered the corpse. I cut off the head and the arms and the legs.

The NARRATOR replaces the boards, pressing each seam flush with the floor.

(SOUND: floorboards knock softly into place; the bell tolls four)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I replaced the boards so cleverly, so cunningly, that no human eye—not even his—could have detected anything wrong.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
There was nothing to wash out—no stain of any kind—no blood-spot whatever.

INT. OLD HOUSE HALL - PRE-DAWN

The street door shudders under three deliberate knocks. The NARRATOR smooths his sleeves and opens it.

(SOUND: three knocks echo through the house; rain drips from the eaves)

Three silent POLICE OFFICERS stand at the threshold in dark coats and wet hats.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
A shriek had been heard by a neighbour during the night. Suspicion of foul play had been aroused.

The NARRATOR ushers the officers inside with an expansive gesture.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I bade the gentlemen welcome. The shriek, I said, was my own in a dream.

INT. OLD MAN'S BEDCHAMBER - PRE-DAWN

The officers inspect the room. The OLD MAN'S treasures remain undisturbed in an open chest. The NARRATOR sets three chairs directly over the concealed floorboards.

(SOUND: chair legs scrape the floorboards; rain fades outside)

The officers sit. The NARRATOR takes the chair centered over the hidden body.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I led them, at length, to his chamber. I showed them his treasures, secure, undisturbed.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
In the wild audacity of my perfect triumph, I placed my own seat upon the very spot beneath which reposed the corpse.

The officers converse quietly among themselves. The NARRATOR smiles too broadly.

(SOUND: low polite murmurs; a faint ringing tone begins)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The officers were satisfied. My manner had convinced them.

The NARRATOR'S smile stiffens. He rubs one temple.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
But, ere long, I felt myself getting pale and wished them gone.

(SOUND: faint, low heartbeat resumes beneath the floor)

The NARRATOR talks faster, gesturing toward the walls, the shutters, the furniture.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The ringing became more distinct. Until, at length, I found that the noise was not within my ears.

The heartbeat grows louder. The officers continue their pleasant conversation, apparently unaware.

(SOUND: heartbeat intensifies under the officers' calm voices)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was a low, dull, quick sound—much such a sound as a watch makes when enveloped in cotton.

The NARRATOR rises and paces with heavy strides across the room.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
I talked more quickly—more vehemently; but the noise steadily increased.

The NARRATOR seizes his chair and drags it violently across the boards.

(SOUND: chair legs grate against wood; heartbeat pounds relentlessly)

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would they not be gone?

The officers smile at one another. The NARRATOR turns on them, shaking.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
They heard!—they suspected!—they knew!—they were making a mockery of my horror!

The NARRATOR points wildly at the floor.

(SOUND: heartbeat thunders; the NARRATOR'S breath breaks into a scream)

NARRATOR
Villains! dissemble no more! I admit the deed!—tear up the planks!—here, here!—It is the beating of his hideous heart!

FADE OUT.
